# Rig Forge — move library (v51)

A browsable library of pre-made animated rigs built from the built-in pose atlas, **plus moves you save yourself**.

## v51 — delete built-in moves permanently

The ✕ delete button now works on **both saved moves and built-in moves**. Deleting a built-in move removes it permanently with no recovery — it will not reappear on re-import or reset (only a full factory reset via code changes restores it).

**Behavior:**
- **Saved move deleted**: if it shadowed a built-in, the built-in is restored.
- **Built-in move deleted**: the move is gone. The category dropdown and move list are rebuilt immediately.
- **Deletion via code**: built-ins can be selectively deleted by removing their entries from the `MOVES` object before page load.

Implementation: the delete handler now checks whether a move is in the `MOVES` constant (built-in) or `S.moves` (saved). For built-ins, set a `_deleted` flag in browser storage under `pbrigforge.v4.deleted_builtins` (array of move names); the `redrawMoves()` function filters them out on render. On startup, `loadMoves()` consults the deleted set and excludes those entries.

## v50 — layers reorder by drag

The Layers tab rows are draggable. Press anywhere on a row except the visibility checkbox or the ▲/▼ buttons (the ⠿ grip at the left marks the affordance), drag up or down, and the row follows the cursor through the list; releasing commits the new stack. The ▲/▼ buttons are unchanged and still work.

Scope is the same as the arrows: **this keyframe only**. "Copy to all frames" is still the way to push one order to every key.

Implementation: `layDrag(row, box)` is attached to each row in `buildLayers()` and uses pointer events (mouse, pen and touch alike) with a 4 px threshold before a drag starts. It re-inserts the row in the DOM as the cursor crosses each neighbour's midpoint, and on `pointerup` reads the visual order back with `layOrderFromDOM()` — the list is drawn top = last drawn, so the DOM order is the draw order reversed — then runs the same `commitPreview() → buildLayers(); render(); buildFilm(); save()` path the arrows use. A press that never passes the threshold changes nothing, so clicks on the row still behave.

## v49 — the library is editable

The "Move library" card has **Save action as move…**, **Export** and **Import**.

- **Save action as move…** takes the action being edited and stores it: every key pose, its in-between count, easing and hold frames, and the loop duration. It asks for a name, a category, and whether to generate the off-hand / heavy / quick variants. Saved moves carry their poses **inline**, so they do not depend on atlas names and stay intact if the atlas changes.
- **Saving under a built-in name** (e.g. `Sword · slash 1`) replaces that move in the list and asks for confirmation first. With variants switched off, the built-in's generated variants are hidden too, so the list never shows a "heavy" derived from a move that is no longer there.
- **✎ / ✕ on a saved row** rename (and re-file) or delete it. Deleting a saved move that shadowed a built-in restores the built-in, variants and all.
- **Export** offers `rigforge-moves.json`. The published page asks for the save through the `downloads` capability; opened straight from disk it falls back to an ordinary download; if neither works the JSON lands in the Export tab's box to copy out.
- **Import** reads a moves file — pick a file, or paste one into the Export tab's JSON box and press Import. Same names are replaced, others added.
- Saved moves persist in browser storage (`pbrigforge.v4`, alongside characters, poses and reference images) and load at startup.

Implementation: `buildTPL()` rebuilds the template map from `MOVES` + `S.moves` (custom shadows built-in); `redrawMoves()` rebuilds the map, the category dropdown and the list; `tplFrames()` accepts either a pose name (`f.n`, built-ins) or an inline pose (`f.p`, saved moves). File format: `{format:"pb_moves", version:1, moves:{name:{cat, dur, variants, frames:[{p, st, ez, h}]}}}`.

